00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.220
Now when we start talking
about the Google Cloud,

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.650
and one thing I always like
to mention to everyone,

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.040
make sure everyone's aware, is
that when you put your data up

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.920
into our cloud, we
always, always encrypt it.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.730
There's no such thing as
unencrypted data at rest.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.260
And there's nothing that you
can turn off or do anything

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:49.810
like that.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.570
It's just something
that we do and there's

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:52.980
no choice around it.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.200
Now the data gets
uploaded to Google.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.660
And what we do is we go through
this very elaborate process

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.364
to actually protect your data
in several different ways.

00:04:00.364 --> 00:04:02.280
The first thing we do
is that we take the data

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.519
and we break it up
into small chunks.

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:06.810
You can think of them as
shards, or blocks, or whatever

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:08.220
you want to think
of, but they're

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:09.770
broken up into small chunks.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:13.280
And for each of those chunks,
we generate a random unique AS

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.040
key.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.330
And so you may ask,
why do you do that?

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:19.829
One of the obvious
reason is that it

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:21.300
reduces the blast radius.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.380
So if one of these
keys gets compromised,

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:25.230
then you don't
lose all your data.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.150
You lose a small fragment of it.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:28.680
And in order to
actually get access

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.721
to the complete set of
data, you have to actually

00:04:30.721 --> 00:04:33.480
be able to break all these
different keys, which

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.460
is actually not a
trivial thing to do.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:36.800
Right.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.040
So each data, each chunk of
data gets its own encrypted key.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.020
And this encrypted
chunks of data

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.941
gets distributed
throughout the data center.

00:04:46.941 --> 00:04:47.440
OK.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.550
They get replicated and
they get moved around.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:54.700
And so again, this also helps
from a security perspective.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.076
A lost hard drive
this is likely not

00:04:57.076 --> 00:04:58.700
going to lead to
anything catastrophic.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:04:59.199
Right.

00:04:59.199 --> 00:05:01.490
Your data is not all
put in one place.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:03.950
It's broken up and distributed
across a data center.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:07.380
And our data center has many,
many, many, many, many, many,

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.590
many servers, lots and lots.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:11.820
And so that's what
happens there.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.600
Now when you start
talking about encryption,

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.356
then it's not the
actual process of doing

00:05:16.356 --> 00:05:17.480
the encryption that's hard.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:18.620
It's the key management.

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:19.120
Right?

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:20.150
Everybody knows this.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:24.330
I mean protecting keys is a
very, very hard thing to do.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.330
And because no matter
how good your corruption

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:30.260
is, if someone takes a key,
then they access your data.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:31.670
Simple as that.

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:34.430
In our case, what we do
is that, when you actually

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:38.780
have that piece of data,
we create this new key

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:40.800
that's associated with it.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.170
Now that key actually
stays with the data itself.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:45.470
And you obviously don't want
to do that in plaintext,

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:49.070
because then it's even
stupider than unencrypting.

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:50.870
But we then wrap it.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:53.630
We encrypt it using a
higher level key that we

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:55.160
call the key encryption key.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:56.560
So it makes sense, right?

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.060
Data encryption key for the
data and then key encryption key

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:00.115
for encrypting the data--

00:06:00.115 --> 00:06:00.740
I mean the key.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:01.730
I'm sorry.

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:04.782
And the way that this works
is that the key encryption

00:06:04.782 --> 00:06:08.365
key resides in our own
internal key management system.

00:06:08.365 --> 00:06:09.740
And this key
management system is

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:11.300
designed to be very
highly scalable,

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:12.424
available, everything else.

00:06:12.424 --> 00:06:14.330
It's basically what
we use internally

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:17.450
to run Google as well,
so it had to be something

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:21.410
that can perform well enough
to support the entire Google

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:23.510
domain.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.060
When the key actually
gets protected in there,

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:30.800
then the internal KMS also has
properties by which it actually

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.680
makes sure that the
proper ACL is involved,

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.569
you know like the services have
the proper access to access key

00:06:37.569 --> 00:06:38.610
for this particular data.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:41.770
And I'll talk a little
more about that as well.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:45.655
But let's walk through what
happens when a service accesses

00:06:45.655 --> 00:06:47.474
an encrypted piece of data.

00:06:47.474 --> 00:06:49.890
So first thing that happens
is that you have three parties

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:50.160
here.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:51.868
You have the service,
the storage system,

00:06:51.868 --> 00:06:53.840
and the actual key
management system.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:56.970
They all have to work
together to make things work.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:00.390
The service comes on and says
hey, I want this piece of data.

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:01.724
Please give it to me.

00:07:01.724 --> 00:07:04.140
And so what the storage system
does is that, first of all,

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:05.760
it checks does the
person actually

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:07.780
have access to this data.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.200
And if it passes that
IM check, then it says,

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.640
OK, let me go and figure
out how to do this.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:15.960
It will go and figure out
which chunks collectively

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:17.400
form that data.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.010
And then each of those
chunks will actually

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:23.820
have its data encryption
keys sent over to the KMS

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:26.400
to actually go and decrypt
so that it can actually

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:27.539
decrypt the data.

00:07:27.539 --> 00:07:29.580
So it does that for each
of the different chunks.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:34.020
And then once it gets back the
unwrapped data encryption key,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.760
it can then actually put
the data back together

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.221
and then send it back
to the application.

00:07:39.221 --> 00:07:41.470
I should mention, too, if
you guys have any questions,

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:42.810
please feel free to interrupt.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.560
I'd rather ask right now.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:45.060
Yes.

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:45.560
Please.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.532
AUDIENCE: You said everything's
encrypted when at rest but then

00:07:48.532 --> 00:07:50.323
the documentation says
some exceptions that

00:07:50.323 --> 00:07:51.960
come with the logs.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:53.650
IL-SUNG LEE: Yeah.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:57.210
So the question
is is that I said

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:59.050
that all data is
encrypted at rest,

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:00.960
but there's documentation
of some things that

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:02.050
are exceptions.

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:02.890
So I should clarify.

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:04.940
When I say data, I
mean customer data.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:08.110
We make a distinction between
customer data and metadata.

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:11.800
And things like logs and
information around the data

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.209
is considered metadata,
which we don't necessarily

00:08:14.209 --> 00:08:15.250
ensure they're encrypted.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:17.121
AUDIENCE: Well,
console logs, if you

00:08:17.121 --> 00:08:19.287
were thinking of an application
that sends something

00:08:19.287 --> 00:08:23.020
to the console logs, so
that would be [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:24.586
IL-SUNG LEE: That's
true, but we try

00:08:24.586 --> 00:08:25.960
to make sure that
there's nothing

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:27.709
in terms of sensitive
data that's actually

00:08:27.709 --> 00:08:29.030
placed into these logs.

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:29.530
Right.

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:32.080
Now if you actually do
something where you actually

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.500
name certain objects with
the sensitive information,

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:37.179
we advise you not to do that.

00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:39.710
But again, we make
that distinction.

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:40.960
And so you're right.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.500
But we cannot always encrypt the
metadata for various reasons.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:47.000
OK.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.420
So now we get down
to a little bit more

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:53.980
in terms of key hierarchy.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.140
And so this diagram
here actually gives you

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:59.740
a fairly good picture of
how we protect the keys

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:03.130
within the storage system.

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:04.852
And so once we actually
get to the top,

00:09:04.852 --> 00:09:07.060
we've already discussed that
where they have the keys

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:10.960
encrypted and the internal
KMS actually is protecting

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:12.624
the green keys there.

00:09:12.624 --> 00:09:14.290
It goes through a
whole elaborate system

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:16.900
of different keys that
actually protect it

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:19.080
through different
layers to a point where,

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.840
at the second from the
bottom right there,

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:25.490
you end up with a
root KMS master key.

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:28.140
And this is a key that runs on
very, very special machines,

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:31.180
very highly secured machines
in each of the data centers.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:32.431
There's not very many of them.

00:09:32.431 --> 00:09:32.930
OK.

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:34.780
They're designed to
be very, very reliable

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:36.500
and performant and
everything else.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.680
And they are also meant
to not store the keys

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:39.980
on any permanent media.

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:40.480
OK.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:41.804
So they only exist in memory.

00:09:41.804 --> 00:09:43.720
And there's an internal
protocol that actually

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:44.800
shares the keys around.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:47.060
So as long as you have one
of these machines running

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:50.020
globally, then you'll be
able to recover in case

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:51.700
any of these machines die.

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.280
Now in the case where we
have a catastrophic failure,

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:59.870
like everything goes
down, like a global reset,

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:03.670
then we do actually keep
a hard copy of that key.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:06.040
We put it into a
physically secure media

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.020
and lock them into a
physical safe that's

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:12.610
distributed in a globally
disparate set of locations

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:13.930
around the world.

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:16.030
And there are very, very
few people in Google

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:18.309
that actually have access
or knowledge of this.

00:10:18.309 --> 00:10:19.600
I'm definitely not one of them.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.370
I'm not considered
trustworthy enough.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:25.634
But there's probably less than
20 if I remember correctly.

00:10:25.634 --> 00:10:28.590
OK.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:31.650
Now just as a general statement
that I'll make around this is

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.050
that this--

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:36.450
the way we can describe
encryption at rest by default

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.959
is Google encrypts customer
content stored at rest

00:10:38.959 --> 00:10:41.250
without any action required
from the customer using one

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.180
or more encryption mechanisms.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:43.770
OK.

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:46.170
So what I describe right
there is one of the mechanisms

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.032
that we use to actually
encrypt your data.

00:10:48.032 --> 00:10:50.490
There's sometimes more at the
physical layer and everything

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:51.120
else.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.349
But you can rest assured
that there's at least--

00:10:53.349 --> 00:10:55.140
your data has been
encrypted at least once.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:10:59.208
Yes.

00:10:59.208 --> 00:11:03.080
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
at some point,

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.984
do you instantiate
that key [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:05.984 --> 00:11:07.436
bootstrap the system.

00:11:07.436 --> 00:11:10.060
Can you go into how you
did that [INAUDIBLE]??

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:11.810
IL-SUNG LEE: So the
question is, how do we

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:14.470
instantiate the key that's
bootstrapped into memory?

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:15.730
Right.

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:18.920
So again, as long as one
of these nodes are up,

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:20.545
and so you have these
specialized nodes

00:11:20.545 --> 00:11:23.770
that only actually serve
to actually provide

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:28.040
this hostess root
KMS master key.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.610
So there is basically
a sharing protocol

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:34.340
that's inherent with
the system itself.

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:36.507
And so they know how to
actually contact one another

00:11:36.507 --> 00:11:39.048
to actually make sure that the
key gets shared and propagated

00:11:39.048 --> 00:11:40.340
throughout the system.

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:43.530
And so if there's a new
machine that comes up then

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:48.190
in automatically gets shared
for the brand new key.

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:50.740
It's a little more
elaborate than that.

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:52.720
I can't get into too
much more details.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:53.470
But, yes?

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:58.340
AUDIENCE: Initially, [INAUDIBLE]
can you initially instantiate

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:01.750
that first node [INAUDIBLE]?

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:03.470
IL-SUNG LEE: Oh I see.

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:07.060
So when you start from zero and
you actually want to actually--

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:09.590
I'm not sure about that,
to be quite honest.

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:12.350
I imagine that there's some
process by which the few people

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:15.530
who knew how to do this would
set up and get it started,

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:17.240
but I'm not exactly sure.

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:18.790
Yes, question?

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:23.245
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
receive the data

00:12:23.245 --> 00:12:29.092
from one of the servers,
or is [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:29.092 --> 00:12:30.050
IL-SUNG LEE: I'm sorry.

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:31.450
Could you repeat that?

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:35.402
AUDIENCE: Is there-- when you
said that there is a unique ID

00:12:35.402 --> 00:12:40.342
[INAUDIBLE] matching between
the different pieces of data,

00:12:40.342 --> 00:12:41.330
right, or [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:42.038
IL-SUNG LEE: Yes.

00:12:42.038 --> 00:12:46.270
AUDIENCE: That means I don't
need to [INAUDIBLE] pieces

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:47.512
ID came from?

00:12:47.512 --> 00:12:48.470
IL-SUNG LEE: Oh, I see.

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:50.800
So the question is basically
around traceability

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:53.410
of where the data resided.

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:54.850
Yes.

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:57.322
There's both internal
logs that actually

00:12:57.322 --> 00:12:58.780
keep track of all
the activity that

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:02.020
happens in terms of key
access and everything else.

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:03.730
And then at a higher
level, there's

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:08.020
also the cloud auto logging
that would actually-- actually,

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:09.380
that's not for this case.

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:10.420
But there is
actually logging that

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:12.340
actually keeps track of
the different accesses

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:16.330
and the different key
requests and everything else.

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:19.420
That's all actual recorded
in our internal logs.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:19.920
OK.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:21.565
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:21.565 --> 00:13:22.523
IL-SUNG LEE: I'm sorry.

00:13:22.523 --> 00:13:24.690
AUDIENCE: Will it
appear in the metadata?

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:26.860
IL-SUNG LEE: Will
appear in the metadata?

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:31.420
Like do you mean if someone
accesses the metadata?

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:32.510
Yeah.

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:36.520
There would be logs in
case that any key access.

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:37.024
Right.

00:13:37.024 --> 00:13:38.440
Well, it's not
necessary something

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:41.769
that's exposed externally.

00:13:41.769 --> 00:13:44.060
Going forward here, so now
we actually get to the point

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:46.143
where we're starting to
talk about things that you

00:13:46.143 --> 00:13:47.370
can manage yourself.

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:49.550
So this is options for
encryption at rest.

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:52.904
And so when you actually bring
up your data to the cloud,

00:13:52.904 --> 00:13:54.320
we discuss the
first column there,

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:56.840
which is a default encryption
where you can actually

00:13:56.840 --> 00:13:58.850
go and do nothing
and we'll just make

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:00.800
sure all your data's encrypted.

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:03.200
And in the middle we
have the customer managed

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:05.370
encryption keys with Cloud KMS.

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:05.900
Right.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:09.050
And that's the case where Cloud
KMS is the cloud service that's

00:14:09.050 --> 00:14:12.060
sort of the external
instantiation

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:14.070
of the key management system.

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:16.550
And one of the great,
great benefits of the KMS

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:20.150
is that you can actually have
this integrated to protect

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:21.210
with different services.

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:23.510
So currently-- I'll talk
a little more about this,

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:25.810
but if your data resides
in other services,

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:27.560
then you can actually
associate with a key

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:29.810
that you control
under Cloud KMS.

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:31.250
And then finally
on the right side

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:33.560
we have customer
supplied encryption keys.

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:37.310
And so this is a feature that we
shipped a couple of years ago.

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:38.930
And this allows
you to actually--

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:42.530
it's only available for GCS and
GCE I should mention as well.

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:47.150
But this allows you to actually
keep the keys resident so you

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:48.587
don't store them in the cloud.

00:14:48.587 --> 00:14:50.420
But every time you
actually make a call that

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:53.720
needs access data, you actually
provide a secret that helps

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:54.905
unlock that data.

00:14:54.905 --> 00:14:56.780
Now that secret is
something that we actually

00:14:56.780 --> 00:14:57.650
only keep in memory.

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.600
We don't actually
store to disc ever.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:01.650
And so as soon as you
finish your request,

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:03.050
we actually purge it.

00:15:03.050 --> 00:15:05.510
Right.

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:09.210
It's only for GCS and GC
as I mentioned before.

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:10.850
It does have some
drawbacks, such

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:15.920
as you can't do anything that's
offline or, like for example,

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:18.559
we can't do any kind
of backups or things

00:15:18.559 --> 00:15:20.100
like compaction and
things like that.

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:21.933
So it limits what we
can do with it in terms

00:15:21.933 --> 00:15:23.718
of the different services.

00:15:23.718 --> 00:15:26.040
OK.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:28.090
Now I'll talk really
quickly about Cloud KMS

00:15:28.090 --> 00:15:29.610
since I mentioned it earlier.

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:34.686
So Cloud KMS is actually the
external key management system.

00:15:34.686 --> 00:15:36.310
And this is all taken
from the website,

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:39.280
so I won't spend too
much time on this.

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:39.840
Right.

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.820
But it basically
gives you a path

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:45.030
by which you can actually
go and create AES 256 keys

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:47.310
and you can manage
them yourselves.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:48.780
You can explicitly
say that I want

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:53.640
to create, destroy, rotate,
disable, whatever you like,

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:54.540
with this.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:57.270
And because it's
a cloud service,

00:15:57.270 --> 00:16:00.120
it's completely integrated
with Cloud IAM and Cloud

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:00.990
Auto-logging.

00:16:00.990 --> 00:16:03.650
So that means you can actually
use IAM roles to actually

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:06.580
dictate who can access the
key and what operations they

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:07.360
can do.

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:08.970
And in addition,
all the operations

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:11.709
will be captured by
Cloud Auto-logging.

00:16:11.709 --> 00:16:13.250
So whenever someone
goes and accesses

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:15.210
the keys for whatever
operation, then that

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:17.126
would be something that's
captured by the log.

00:16:20.689 --> 00:16:22.480
You know, this is just
basically describing

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:24.855
some of the things that if
you're familiar with Cloud KMS

00:16:24.855 --> 00:16:25.780
you already know.

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:27.190
But you know, it's
designed to be

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:30.220
a global scalable
performance system.

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:31.190
It's extremely fast.

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:33.590
Very, very low overhead
just because of the way

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:36.690
that it works is that there's
many, many KMS nodes that

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:37.730
do a lot of the work.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:40.810
And so it means that when
you actually do encryption,

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:43.540
it actually doesn't
really meaningfully impact

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:46.890
the performance of the system.

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:49.189
And then this is
something that's probably

00:16:49.189 --> 00:16:50.480
interesting to a lot of people.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:52.100
What's new?

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:53.120
Asymmetric keys.

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:56.011
We are finally introducing
asymmetric keys for Cloud KMS.

00:16:56.011 --> 00:16:56.510
OK.

00:16:56.510 --> 00:16:59.420
So the other slide a
couple of slides ago,

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:02.769
I said it does a symmetric key.

00:17:02.769 --> 00:17:04.310
That was the case
until this morning.

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:07.609
We just announced that
we're providing Cloud HSM.

00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:10.794
along with Cloud HSM, we're also
providing the ability for you

00:17:10.794 --> 00:17:13.460
to actually go and do asymmetric
key operations on both hardware

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:14.960
and software.

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:17.780
And we're allowing you to
do several different types

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:18.450
of keys.

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:18.950
OK.

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:22.060
So you can actually
us RSA 2K, 3K, and 4K.

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:23.510
You can use that
both for signing

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:25.369
and you can use
that for decryption.

00:17:25.369 --> 00:17:28.550
And then we'll also provide
you with elliptic-curve keys

00:17:28.550 --> 00:17:31.940
with a couple of the NIST
curves, P356 and 384.

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:35.440
So you can use those
for signing purposes.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:40.100
We've also expanded since
last year to multiple regions.

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:42.490
Very recently we expanded to
Finland, London, Frankfurt,

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:46.750
Netherlands, and then Tokyo,
Mumbai, Sydney, Montreal, LA,

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:48.490
and Sao Paulo.

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:49.720
OK.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:52.300
Another thing that's really
cool is that we are now

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:53.990
supporting multi-regions.

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:56.274
So if you're familiar, we
had a multi-region before,

00:17:56.274 --> 00:17:57.440
but it was one multi-region.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:58.391
It was global.

00:17:58.391 --> 00:17:58.890
Right.

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:00.940
So either you chose a
specific region or you

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:02.980
chose the whole entire world.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:05.149
And for some reason
some people didn't

00:18:05.149 --> 00:18:07.690
like that so we're providing
you with the ability to actually

00:18:07.690 --> 00:18:09.500
choose individual regions.

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:12.891
So you can either choose Europe,
Asia, or US multi-regions.

00:18:12.891 --> 00:18:13.390
OK.

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:15.290
And it's just basically
a very, very simple thing

00:18:15.290 --> 00:18:16.498
when you create the key ring.

00:18:18.661 --> 00:18:20.910
And finally, I just mentioned
this a little while ago,

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:25.070
but where we're providing
HSM backing for Cloud KMS.

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:25.840
OK.

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:28.267
So this is a very,
very fantastic news.

00:18:28.267 --> 00:18:30.850
I've been working very hard on
this for the last couple years.

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:32.516
So it's been my life
for a little while,

00:18:32.516 --> 00:18:36.110
so I feel very, very happy
and sad at the same time.

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:40.150
But it's basically a
managed HSM service

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:43.300
that allows you to actually
go and do crypt operations,

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:47.925
create protection of your keys
in a FIPS 140-2 level 3 device.

00:18:47.925 --> 00:18:50.050
And if you think that's
going to be hard to manage,

00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:52.030
well, you don't have
to do anything except

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:53.592
know how to use Cloud KMS.

00:18:53.592 --> 00:18:55.550
And it's just a simple
flag when you create it.

00:18:55.550 --> 00:18:57.470
You say, I want to
create this in HSM.

00:18:57.470 --> 00:18:59.880
And we just take care of the
rest for you essentially.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:01.654
OK.

00:19:01.654 --> 00:19:03.070
It's an HSM, which
means that when

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:05.980
you create the keys in there, we
can't extract the key material.

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:06.480
OK.

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:08.560
We actually really don't
have the capabilities

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:09.760
of actually doing it.

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:14.170
And the keys are actually
bound to a region itself.

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:17.140
When you create the keys
in, say, US central one,

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:20.020
then the HSM hardware
in US central one

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:23.030
is the thing that creates and
binds the keys to that region.

00:19:23.030 --> 00:19:25.240
So even if the key
backup gets moved around,

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:27.060
it can't be used anywhere else.

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:27.640
OK.

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:31.090
I have a whole session on
this tomorrow at 10:20.

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:33.400
And so if you're interested
about this, please drop by.

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:36.460
I'll talk a lot more about
the HSM stuff and do a lot

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:39.850
more demos and stuff like that
where you can see how it works.

00:19:39.850 --> 00:19:42.820
OK.

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:43.340
All right.

00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:45.320
I'm going to-- I see
people taking pictures

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:47.500
so three, two, one.

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:48.630
OK.

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:51.240
And finally, customer
managed encryption keys.

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:53.910
If you guys haven't
used this yet,

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:55.260
you should really consider it.

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:56.440
It's great.

00:19:56.440 --> 00:19:59.340
It essentially allows
you to use the encryption

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:01.220
that we provide into
the different services

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:04.810
but protect it using a key
that you control in Cloud KMS.

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:05.460
Right.

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:08.610
And because Cloud HSM is
integrated with Cloud KMS,

00:20:08.610 --> 00:20:10.995
you can also use
an HSM key as well

00:20:10.995 --> 00:20:12.870
if you feel like having
some tighter controls

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:15.960
around the ability of the keys.

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:17.580
And so currently
right now we have

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:21.240
this available for
BigQuery that's fully GA.

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:23.400
The Compute Engine,
cloud storage, data proc,

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:24.650
they're all beta right now.

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:27.900
And so you're free to try this
with both software and hardware

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:29.190
keys.

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:32.670
There's a talk release for the
GCS happening tomorrow Thursday

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:35.320
at 10:20 AM, same time.

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:38.940
I hear the HSM talk is
a little bit better.

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:40.530
But if you're
interested in GCS, then

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:42.960
please feel free to actually
go and attend this session,

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:43.680
learn a little bit more.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:46.138
I don't think they're going to
talk exclusively about this,

00:20:46.138 --> 00:20:47.970
but they'll mention it.

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:50.270
And with that, I think
I'm going to turn it over

