00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:26.477
familiar with that.

00:21:26.477 --> 00:21:28.810
So I'll we talking about
encryption in transit at Google

00:21:28.810 --> 00:21:30.880
and how that works.

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:33.630
So if you're not familiar,
and to understand

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:35.380
how encryption in
transit works at Google,

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:37.180
we first need to
understand how traffic

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:39.652
moves to Google and
then inside Google.

00:21:39.652 --> 00:21:41.860
So there are five kinds of
different running requests

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:43.360
here, they're
numbered on the slide,

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:45.640
from the end user
to a Google service,

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:48.820
from the end user to a custom
application hosted on Google,

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:52.090
from a virtual machine
to a Google service,

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:54.550
from a virtual machine to
another virtual machine,

00:21:54.550 --> 00:21:56.230
and from a service
to another service.

00:21:56.230 --> 00:21:57.646
So the distinction
here, a service

00:21:57.646 --> 00:22:00.846
is a hosted service like
a BigQuery or Cloud ML,

00:22:00.846 --> 00:22:02.470
et cetera versus a
VM is a VM endpoint.

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:05.050
It has an IP address
like GCE, GKE,

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:09.700
or a Google managed
VM like Cloud SQL is.

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:11.920
So number one here from
the end user to Google,

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:13.890
to a Google Cloud
service, for example,

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:16.480
if a user's connecting to
BigQuery to run a query.

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:18.460
Google Cloud services
accept requests

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:20.543
from around the world using
a globally distributed

00:22:20.543 --> 00:22:23.200
system called the
Google front end or GFE.

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:26.590
And the GSE will terminate
incoming HTTPS, TCP,

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:29.870
and TLS proxy traffic, provided
DDoS and tech countermeasures,

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:32.680
and route and load balanced
traffic to the right Google

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:33.685
Cloud Service.

00:22:33.685 --> 00:22:36.770
There are GFE points of
presence around the globe.

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:38.530
So they proxy that
traffic internally

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:41.200
by writing the user's request
over our network backbone

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:43.010
to the right spot.

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:45.310
Two, from the end user
to a customer application

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:46.720
hosted on Google Cloud.

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:48.730
This is actually the
most complicated case,

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:52.170
so hopefully we don't have to--

00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:54.470
I can talk through some of
the examples that you have.

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:56.200
So this depends on
how your traffic

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:58.700
is routed through the internet
to your customer application.

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:00.574
If you're using a Google
Cloud load balancer,

00:23:00.574 --> 00:23:04.300
you can use it to terminate
HTTPS, TLS, and TCP traffic.

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:06.730
And it's actually
implemented by the GFEs.

00:23:06.730 --> 00:23:10.610
So that's why you have the same
parallel set of options there.

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:11.869
For HTTPS load balancers.

00:23:11.869 --> 00:23:14.410
Connections between end users
are encrypted and authenticated

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:18.010
with TLS or [? Quick ?] using
certificates that you provide.

00:23:18.010 --> 00:23:21.940
Some other options if you're
connecting using Cloud VPN,

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:24.220
you connect from
your VM on premises

00:23:24.220 --> 00:23:28.360
to the on prem VPN to the
cloud VPN to the cloud VN.

00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:30.730
And the connection
between the two VPNs

00:23:30.730 --> 00:23:32.560
is protected using IPsec.

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:34.570
And we'll talk a
bit more about that.

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:39.640
If you're connecting directly
to an external IP for a VM,

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:42.177
that connection does
not go through the GFE

00:23:42.177 --> 00:23:44.260
and it is not encrypted
and protected the same way

00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:45.520
that a connection to the GFE is.

00:23:45.520 --> 00:23:47.780
So if you're serving something
directly from an IPN point,

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:49.821
that's your responsibility
to protect and encrypt

00:23:49.821 --> 00:23:50.530
that traffic.

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:53.290
Similarly, if using cloud
dedicated interconnect,

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:55.330
for example to peer
between your on premises

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:58.304
and Google Cloud, that also
does not go through the GFE

00:23:58.304 --> 00:24:00.220
and so it's also you're
responsible to protect

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:02.800
that, for example by
implementing some application

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:05.050
layer encryption.

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:06.460
From a virtual machine.

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:07.670
So that was two.

00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:09.220
For three, from
a virtual machine

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:11.530
to a Google Cloud
service, it's actually

00:24:11.530 --> 00:24:13.330
just reduces to
another case, which

00:24:13.330 --> 00:24:16.500
is it will connect to a GFE.

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:19.270
We will route that request, for
example from a Compute Engine

00:24:19.270 --> 00:24:23.029
VM to Google Cloud Storage, as
if it were an external request

00:24:23.029 --> 00:24:24.070
and gets sent to the GFE.

00:24:24.070 --> 00:24:26.319
Then GFE will then do their
load balancing and routing

00:24:26.319 --> 00:24:27.769
internally for that.

00:24:27.769 --> 00:24:29.560
From a virtual machine
to a virtual machine

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:33.580
that connects over our network
backbone using private RFC 1918

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:34.376
IP addresses.

00:24:34.376 --> 00:24:37.000
And from a Google Cloud service
to another Google Cloud service

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:38.740
also goes over our
private network backbone.

00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:41.230
We'll talk about what we do
for both of those in a second.

00:24:45.020 --> 00:24:46.150
Here we go.

00:24:46.150 --> 00:24:50.870
To get us started here, our
networking team is amazing.

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:53.227
And all the stuff that
you saw there was crazy,

00:24:53.227 --> 00:24:54.310
but I'm a security person.

00:24:54.310 --> 00:24:56.226
So I care about how that
traffic is protected.

00:24:56.226 --> 00:24:59.540
How does it-- all those five
different things are protected.

00:24:59.540 --> 00:25:02.240
So first from the
user to the front end.

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:03.790
It travels over
the open internet.

00:25:03.790 --> 00:25:05.165
And then I'll talk
more about how

00:25:05.165 --> 00:25:07.120
we protect that in a second.

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:10.150
Then once your traffic is
inside Google's infrastructure,

00:25:10.150 --> 00:25:13.230
your data travels over our
private network backbone.

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:14.980
It might go to multiple
physical locations

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:16.870
in order to fulfill
that request.

00:25:16.870 --> 00:25:19.690
So how is our network
backbone protected?

00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:22.720
It's really important here to
understand that Google applies

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:24.640
different protections
to data in transit

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:27.730
when it's transmitted outside of
a physical boundary controlled

00:25:27.730 --> 00:25:29.320
by or on behalf
of Google compared

00:25:29.320 --> 00:25:32.000
to when it stays inside
Google's physical boundaries.

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:33.400
So what do I mean by this?

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:35.530
A physical boundary
is the barrier

00:25:35.530 --> 00:25:38.214
to a physical space that is
controlled by or on behalf

00:25:38.214 --> 00:25:39.880
of Google where we
can ensure that there

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:42.784
are rigorous security
measures in place.

00:25:42.784 --> 00:25:44.200
Physical access
to these locations

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.279
is restricted and
heavily monitored.

00:25:46.279 --> 00:25:48.070
Only a small percentage
of Google employees

00:25:48.070 --> 00:25:50.350
can have access
to that hardware.

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:52.580
And because we tightly
control that space,

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:54.730
data in transit within
these physical boundaries

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:58.310
is generally authenticated but
is not necessarily encrypted.

00:25:58.310 --> 00:26:01.940
Now compare that to the scale of
the global internet, everything

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:03.487
in our wide area network.

00:26:03.487 --> 00:26:05.320
We just can't put the
same physical security

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:05.920
controls in place.

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:06.420
Right.

00:26:06.420 --> 00:26:08.490
We can't put a Google
employee standing every 10

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:10.800
feet of some fiber optic
cable under the sea

00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:13.240
making sure that
that's all secured.

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:15.680
So anywhere outside of
our physical boundaries

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:17.790
where we can't
control that link,

00:26:17.790 --> 00:26:20.700
we assume that any network
crossing a physical boundary

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:23.670
can be compromised by
an active adversary who

00:26:23.670 --> 00:26:26.430
could snoop, inject, or
alter traffic on that wire.

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:28.860
In order to ensure data
integrity and privacy,

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:30.300
we use encryption
when data moves

00:26:30.300 --> 00:26:32.340
outside physical boundaries.

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:35.114
So those undersea network
cables, encrypted.

00:26:35.114 --> 00:26:37.530
I want to repeat this because
it's really the key takeaway

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:39.660
here before I get into details.

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:41.871
Based on our threat
model, Google

00:26:41.871 --> 00:26:43.620
encrypts and authenticates
data in transit

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:46.380
when that data moves
outside physical boundaries,

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:48.320
but whereas data within
a physical boundary

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:51.191
is generally authenticated
but not always encrypted.

00:26:51.191 --> 00:26:51.690
All right.

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:52.950
So now that we have
the high level picture,

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:55.369
let's talk through some of
the different scenarios,

00:26:55.369 --> 00:26:56.910
both to the Google
front end and then

00:26:56.910 --> 00:27:00.390
within Google's infrastructure
at multiple network layers.

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:01.917
So as I mentioned
briefly earlier,

00:27:01.917 --> 00:27:03.750
your data control from
the user to the front

00:27:03.750 --> 00:27:05.170
in a few different ways.

00:27:05.170 --> 00:27:08.790
Let's take the most simple
case where you're a user

00:27:08.790 --> 00:27:10.541
and you connect to a
Google Cloud service.

00:27:10.541 --> 00:27:12.873
So in this case, the Google
front end, like I mentioned,

00:27:12.873 --> 00:27:15.240
will terminate your connection
and provide encryption

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:18.420
using HTTPS protocol with
a public certificate.

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:21.174
The GFE will negotiate a
particular encryption protocol

00:27:21.174 --> 00:27:22.590
with the client,
depending on what

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:24.120
the client's able to support.

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:27.000
The GFE will negotiate a more
modern encryption protocol

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:28.800
where possible.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:31.620
TLS in the GFE is
implemented using BoringSSL,

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:33.590
which is a Google
maintained open source

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:36.870
implementation of the TLS
protocol forked from OpenSSL

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:39.550
and is mostly interface
compatible with OpenSSL.

00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:42.990
Google's an industry leader
in both the adoption of TLS

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:45.180
and the strengthening
of its implementation.

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:48.120
We've enabled many of the
security features of TLS.

00:27:48.120 --> 00:27:51.210
For example, since 2011, we
have been using forward secrecy

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:52.890
in our TLS implementation.

00:27:52.890 --> 00:27:55.380
Forward secrecy makes
sure that the key that

00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:57.640
protects a particular
connection is not persistent

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:00.720
so that if that connection is
compromised, if an attacker can

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:02.264
intercept a particular
message, they

00:28:02.264 --> 00:28:03.930
can't read previous
messages that you've

00:28:03.930 --> 00:28:06.390
sent over the same connection.

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:09.900
As part of TLS, a server must
prove its identity to the user

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:11.970
when it receives the
connection request

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:14.430
by having the server present
a certificate containing

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:15.960
its claimed identity.

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:18.570
This certificate is signed by
an issuing certificate authority

00:28:18.570 --> 00:28:22.920
or CA that's trusted by the
user requesting the encryption.

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:25.980
Historically, Google
operated its own issuing CA,

00:28:25.980 --> 00:28:27.689
which is what gives
you the certificates.

00:28:27.689 --> 00:28:29.229
And we use these to
sign certificates

00:28:29.229 --> 00:28:30.090
for Google domains.

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:32.040
But recently
Google's also started

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:33.760
operating our own root CA.

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:36.330
So the root CAs sign the
other smaller CAs that

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.010
operate across the internet.

00:28:38.010 --> 00:28:39.750
Today our CA serves
certificates that

00:28:39.750 --> 00:28:42.930
are cross-signed by multiple
routes which are ubiquitously

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:44.400
distributed.

00:28:44.400 --> 00:28:46.800
So to summarize, when
you connect to Google,

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:49.140
the Google front end
terminates your traffic,

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:51.165
encryption is implemented
using BoringSSL,

00:28:51.165 --> 00:28:53.220
and certificates are
cross-signed including

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:56.640
by Google owned root CAs.

00:28:56.640 --> 00:28:58.320
Now let's get into our network.

00:28:58.320 --> 00:29:01.200
Now that your request is
inside Google's infrastructure,

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:03.960
I might have to travel
between Google locations.

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:06.910
We have protections in place,
both at the network layer

00:29:06.910 --> 00:29:08.890
and at the application layer.

00:29:08.890 --> 00:29:10.520
Let's start at
the network layer.

00:29:10.520 --> 00:29:12.910
At this layer, Google
Cloud's virtual network

00:29:12.910 --> 00:29:16.170
authenticates all traffic
between virtual machines.

00:29:16.170 --> 00:29:17.710
This authentication
is implemented

00:29:17.710 --> 00:29:20.351
using a system that we
call security tokens, which

00:29:20.351 --> 00:29:22.350
protect a compromised
host from spoofing packets

00:29:22.350 --> 00:29:23.790
on the network.

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:26.791
Security tokens are HMACs
negotiated for a given sender

00:29:26.791 --> 00:29:27.540
and receiver pair.

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:29.498
So a sender VM and a
receiver VM will each have

00:29:29.498 --> 00:29:32.347
their own set of tokens.

00:29:32.347 --> 00:29:33.930
During authentication,
security tokens

00:29:33.930 --> 00:29:35.814
are encapsulated in
a HTML header, which

00:29:35.814 --> 00:29:37.980
contains authentication
information about the sender

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:38.789
and the receiver.

00:29:38.789 --> 00:29:40.330
The control plane
on the serving side

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:43.860
sets the token and the receiving
host validates the token.

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:46.500
Furthermore, Google Cloud's
virtual network infrastructure

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:48.402
enables encryption
when traffic goes

00:29:48.402 --> 00:29:50.610
outside physical boundaries
like I was saying before.

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:52.470
Encryption applies
to private IP traffic

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:54.200
within the same
virtual private cloud

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:56.996
or between peer
and VPC networks.

00:29:56.996 --> 00:29:58.620
We use the advanced
encryption standard

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:02.355
in a Galois Counter
Mode with 128-bit key

00:30:02.355 --> 00:30:04.230
to implement encryption
at the network layer.

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:07.020
Each pair of communicating
hosts establishes a session key

00:30:07.020 --> 00:30:09.840
via a control channel protected
by ALTS, which I'll talk about

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:13.110
in a second, for authenticated
and encrypted communications.

00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:15.907
The session key is to encrypt
all VM to VM communication

00:30:15.907 --> 00:30:16.740
between these hosts.

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:21.130
Going up to the
application layer,

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:23.190
the production story
is very similar

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:24.987
although the implementation
is different.

00:30:24.987 --> 00:30:27.320
At the application layer
within Google's infrastructure,

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:31.160
we use application layer
transport security or ALTS

00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:33.320
for the authentication,
integrity, and encryption

00:30:33.320 --> 00:30:35.120
of Google RPC calls.

00:30:35.120 --> 00:30:37.850
This includes RPC calls
from the GFE to a service

00:30:37.850 --> 00:30:40.530
and from a service
to another service.

00:30:40.530 --> 00:30:43.460
ALTS uses service accounts
for authentication.

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:45.920
Each service that runs in
Google's infrastructure

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:48.770
has a service account identity
with associated cryptographic

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:51.290
credentials which it can use
for authenticating itself

00:30:51.290 --> 00:30:52.730
to other services.

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:55.040
When making or receiving
RPCs from other services,

00:30:55.040 --> 00:30:57.165
it uses that service account
to authenticate itself

00:30:57.165 --> 00:30:58.860
to provide its credentials.

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:00.470
ALTS will verify
these potentials

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:03.290
using an internal
certificate authority.

00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:06.080
Within a physical boundary
controlled by Google,

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:09.350
ALTS provides both
authentication and integrity

00:31:09.350 --> 00:31:10.460
for RPCs.

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:12.800
For traffic over the WAN
outside of physical boundaries,

00:31:12.800 --> 00:31:16.010
ALTS furthermore enforces
encryption for infrastructure

00:31:16.010 --> 00:31:18.610
RPC traffic automatically.

00:31:18.610 --> 00:31:22.460
ALTS is also used to encapsulate
layer 7 protocols such as HTTP.

00:31:22.460 --> 00:31:24.380
This protection
ensures that our--

00:31:24.380 --> 00:31:26.260
this isolates the
application layer

00:31:26.260 --> 00:31:29.480
and removes any dependency
on the network path.

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:34.460
Services can also be configured
to only accept and send ALTS

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:37.190
when encrypted even within
physical boundaries.

00:31:37.190 --> 00:31:40.280
Notable examples within Google
are our internal key management

00:31:40.280 --> 00:31:42.110
system that Il-Sung
was describing earlier

00:31:42.110 --> 00:31:44.240
and Cloud KMS, both
of which only accept

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:46.460
encrypted communications.

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:50.510
Let's dig a little bit
deeper into how ALTS works.

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:53.480
Google's application layer
transport security or ALTS

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:57.560
is a mutual authentication and
transport encryption system

00:31:57.560 --> 00:31:59.480
developed by Google
and typically used

00:31:59.480 --> 00:32:02.300
for securing remote procedure
communications or RPCs

00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:03.960
within our infrastructure.

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:06.410
ALTS is similar in
concept to Mutual TLS

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:08.780
if you're familiar with how
that works, or any handshake

00:32:08.780 --> 00:32:11.270
protocol really, but has
been designed and optimized

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:15.020
to meet the needs of Google's
data center environments.

00:32:15.020 --> 00:32:16.910
The ALTS trust model
was purpose built

00:32:16.910 --> 00:32:19.170
for cloud-like
containerized applications.

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:20.260
This is a key point.

00:32:20.260 --> 00:32:23.540
Identities are bound to
entities like a service account,

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:25.190
not bound to a
specific server name

00:32:25.190 --> 00:32:27.099
or host like an IP address.

00:32:27.099 --> 00:32:28.640
This trust model
makes it really easy

00:32:28.640 --> 00:32:31.340
for us to replicate load
balance and reschedule

00:32:31.340 --> 00:32:33.620
micro services across hosts.

00:32:33.620 --> 00:32:36.448
Sounds a lot like Istio.

00:32:36.448 --> 00:32:39.260
ALTS relies on two protocols,
the handshake protocol

00:32:39.260 --> 00:32:41.720
establishes a session key
which you see at the top there,

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:43.620
and a second protocol,
the record protocol,

00:32:43.620 --> 00:32:45.740
to send that protected RPC.

00:32:45.740 --> 00:32:47.300
These protocols
govern how sessions

00:32:47.300 --> 00:32:50.510
are established, authenticated,
encrypted, and resumed.

00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:51.890
So first the handshake protocol.

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:54.740
It's a Diffie-Hellman based
authentication key exchange

00:32:54.740 --> 00:32:57.140
that supports both perfect
forward secrecy and session

00:32:57.140 --> 00:32:58.100
resumption.

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:00.581
During a handshake, the
client and the server

00:33:00.581 --> 00:33:02.330
will negotiate a shared
transit encryption

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:04.080
key and the specific
protocol that they're

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:06.020
going to use for encryption.

00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:09.296
And then the record protocol
sends the data, encrypts

00:33:09.296 --> 00:33:10.670
and authenticates
network traffic

00:33:10.670 --> 00:33:12.086
using the protocol
secret that was

00:33:12.086 --> 00:33:13.816
established in the handshake.

00:33:13.816 --> 00:33:16.190
We've published a white paper
explaining exactly how ALTS

00:33:16.190 --> 00:33:18.231
works on our website, so
please do check that out

00:33:18.231 --> 00:33:20.400
if you're interested.

00:33:20.400 --> 00:33:22.450
So let's summarize
what we've seen so far.

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:25.710
And apologies for these being
on two different slides.

00:33:25.710 --> 00:33:28.460
So first, what happens at
layer 3, the network layer?

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:32.000
We've covered what protections
we have in place by default.

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:36.020
You know, we mentioned that
we have virtual network

00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:39.680
authentication between the
GFE to the VM and between VMs.

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:41.450
And we have encryption
when that data

00:33:41.450 --> 00:33:43.405
crosses physical boundaries.

00:33:43.405 --> 00:33:44.780
I also want to
point out it's not

00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:46.738
that we don't have any
protection from the user

00:33:46.738 --> 00:33:48.467
at the front end or
from the user to VM,

00:33:48.467 --> 00:33:49.550
but that's not at layer 3.

00:33:49.550 --> 00:33:51.904
That's at layer 7.

00:33:51.904 --> 00:33:53.570
So I get asked often,
is there any point

00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:55.700
where my data isn't
encrypted in transit?

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:56.610
What are my options?

00:33:56.610 --> 00:33:58.340
What can I do?

00:33:58.340 --> 00:34:00.249
At this network
layer, in addition to

00:34:00.249 --> 00:34:01.790
this production that
we already have,

00:34:01.790 --> 00:34:03.165
if you need
additional protection

00:34:03.165 --> 00:34:05.210
from the user to the
VM or between VMs,

00:34:05.210 --> 00:34:08.300
you can use an IPsec tunnel.

00:34:08.300 --> 00:34:10.070
How do you set that up?

00:34:10.070 --> 00:34:12.980
You can use Cloud VPN to set
up an IPsec tunnel securely

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:16.070
connect your on premises network
to your Google Cloud VPC.

00:34:16.070 --> 00:34:17.600
You have the option
of setting up--

00:34:17.600 --> 00:34:20.114
you can also set up a
VPN between two VPCs.

00:34:20.114 --> 00:34:21.530
This means that
your traffic would

00:34:21.530 --> 00:34:25.030
go from your on prem host to
your on prem VM to the Cloud--

00:34:25.030 --> 00:34:28.760
sorry, from your on prem host to
your on prem VPN to your cloud

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:30.679
VPN to your Cloud VM.

00:34:30.679 --> 00:34:33.320
So there's three hops there
that it's making, right?

00:34:33.320 --> 00:34:35.962
From your on prem host
to your on prem VPN,

00:34:35.962 --> 00:34:36.920
that's on your network.

00:34:36.920 --> 00:34:39.211
It's protected like you would
protect any other traffic

00:34:39.211 --> 00:34:40.070
in your environment.

00:34:40.070 --> 00:34:42.590
From the on premises
VPN to the cloud VPN,

00:34:42.590 --> 00:34:45.050
that's protected
using an IPsec tunnel.

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:47.900
And from the cloud
VPN to the Cloud VM,

00:34:47.900 --> 00:34:49.940
that's protected by
our virtual network

00:34:49.940 --> 00:34:52.040
as we previously explained.

00:34:52.040 --> 00:34:53.480
So to set up a
VPN, you first have

00:34:53.480 --> 00:34:56.510
to set up a VPN on premises,
then you can create a cloud

00:34:56.510 --> 00:34:59.840
VPN gateway and tunnel on the
hosted services VPC network,

00:34:59.840 --> 00:35:02.067
and permit traffic
between the networks.

00:35:02.067 --> 00:35:03.650
You can further
customize your network

00:35:03.650 --> 00:35:05.390
by specifying the
Internet Key Exchange

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:07.310
version for your VPN tunnel.

00:35:07.310 --> 00:35:10.790
IKEv1 and IKEv2, each of which
supports different ciphers.

00:35:10.790 --> 00:35:12.770
If you specify
IKEv1, Google will

00:35:12.770 --> 00:35:17.700
encrypt the packets using AES
advanced encryption standard

00:35:17.700 --> 00:35:20.620
in cipher block chaining
mode with 128-bit key

00:35:20.620 --> 00:35:23.442
and provides integrity
using a SHA-1 HMAC.

00:35:23.442 --> 00:35:25.150
If you're using IKEv2,
you have a variety

00:35:25.150 --> 00:35:27.340
of ciphers that are available
to you and supported.

00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:29.410
And in all cases,
Google Cloud VPN

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:31.000
will negotiate the
most secure option

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:33.682
available between
the peer devices.

00:35:33.682 --> 00:35:35.890
An alternative to an IPsec
tunnel I mentioned briefly

00:35:35.890 --> 00:35:37.330
earlier would be
to use something

00:35:37.330 --> 00:35:39.460
like Google Cloud
dedicated interconnect

00:35:39.460 --> 00:35:42.292
to connect from your on
premises to Google Cloud.

00:35:42.292 --> 00:35:44.500
In that case, it provides
direct physical connections

00:35:44.500 --> 00:35:48.460
using RFC 1918 addresses between
your network and Google's

00:35:48.460 --> 00:35:49.205
network.

00:35:49.205 --> 00:35:50.830
The data traveling
over this connection

00:35:50.830 --> 00:35:52.570
is not encrypted
by default and it's

00:35:52.570 --> 00:35:54.720
your protection to secure this.

00:35:54.720 --> 00:35:57.670
It's your responsibility
to secure this

00:35:57.670 --> 00:36:00.430
and we'd recommend, for example,
application layer encryption,

00:36:00.430 --> 00:36:02.926
for example using TLS.

00:36:02.926 --> 00:36:04.300
So that was at
the network level.

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:08.290
Let's look up at the
application layer again.

00:36:08.290 --> 00:36:10.750
As you recall, we use
TLS to encrypt data

00:36:10.750 --> 00:36:12.550
from the user to
the Google front end

00:36:12.550 --> 00:36:14.440
and also from a VM to
the Google front end

00:36:14.440 --> 00:36:17.740
because how we route traffic
back through the GFE.

00:36:17.740 --> 00:36:20.230
And then we use ALTS within
Google's infrastructure

00:36:20.230 --> 00:36:22.910
between services for
authentication, integrity,

00:36:22.910 --> 00:36:26.049
and furthermore encryption
between physical locations.

00:36:26.049 --> 00:36:28.090
To avoid confusion, I also
want to point out here

00:36:28.090 --> 00:36:30.673
that VM to VM traffic, it's not
that it's not protected again,

00:36:30.673 --> 00:36:33.951
it's protected at layer 3.

00:36:33.951 --> 00:36:35.950
If you need additional
network layer protections

00:36:35.950 --> 00:36:38.117
from the user to the
GFE, we have a couple

00:36:38.117 --> 00:36:40.450
of different options, including
Managed SSL certificates

00:36:40.450 --> 00:36:42.140
for your user applications.

00:36:42.140 --> 00:36:43.792
And in Gmail you can use S/MIME.

00:36:43.792 --> 00:36:45.250
And within Google's
infrastructure,

00:36:45.250 --> 00:36:47.590
if you need additional service
to service protections,

00:36:47.590 --> 00:36:48.640
you can Istio.

00:36:48.640 --> 00:36:52.615
So let's quickly dive
into what these provide.

00:36:52.615 --> 00:36:54.490
When building an
application on Google Cloud,

00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:56.404
you can leverage the
GFE support of TLS

00:36:56.404 --> 00:36:57.820
by configuring the
SSL certificate

00:36:57.820 --> 00:36:58.759
that you want to use.

00:36:58.759 --> 00:37:00.550
For example, you could
have the TLS section

00:37:00.550 --> 00:37:02.830
terminate in your application.

00:37:02.830 --> 00:37:05.710
As well, Google provides free
and automated SSL certificates

00:37:05.710 --> 00:37:08.140
in both Firebase Hosting
and Google App Engine custom

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:09.070
domains.

00:37:09.070 --> 00:37:11.361
These certificates are only
available for Google hosted

00:37:11.361 --> 00:37:11.950
properties.

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:14.241
Once your domain is pointed
at Google's infrastructure,

00:37:14.241 --> 00:37:18.640
we request one and obtain a
certificate for that domain

00:37:18.640 --> 00:37:20.255
to allow secure communications.

00:37:20.255 --> 00:37:21.970
We manage these TLS
server private keys

00:37:21.970 --> 00:37:28.630
on your behalf, which are either
2048-bit RSA or SEC P-256 R1

00:37:28.630 --> 00:37:33.300
ECC and renew certificates
on your behalf as well.

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:37.726
For Gmail specifically, Gmail
already uses TLS by default.

00:37:37.726 --> 00:37:40.310
So in fact, Gmail actually
records and displays

00:37:40.310 --> 00:37:42.080
whether the last hop
that an email made

00:37:42.080 --> 00:37:43.850
was over a TLS session.

00:37:43.850 --> 00:37:47.180
When a Gmail user exchanges an
email with another Gmail user,

00:37:47.180 --> 00:37:49.677
the emails are protected
by TLS with the exception

00:37:49.677 --> 00:37:52.010
of if they're sent directly
with the application they're

00:37:52.010 --> 00:37:55.039
protected with ALTS as
we described earlier.

00:37:55.039 --> 00:37:57.080
For incoming messages from
other email providers,

00:37:57.080 --> 00:37:59.480
Gmail doesn't enforce
the use of TLS.

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:02.300
If this is a requirement
for you as a Gmail admin,

00:38:02.300 --> 00:38:04.790
you can go configure Gmail
to require a secure TLS

00:38:04.790 --> 00:38:07.787
connection for all incoming
and outgoing emails.

00:38:07.787 --> 00:38:09.620
If you have stronger
enterprise requirements

00:38:09.620 --> 00:38:11.990
for secure multipurpose
internet mail extensions

00:38:11.990 --> 00:38:15.170
or S/MIME, which is an email
security standard that provides

00:38:15.170 --> 00:38:17.990
authentication, integrity, and
encryption for your emails,

00:38:17.990 --> 00:38:21.249
then you can implement
that in Gmail as well.

00:38:21.249 --> 00:38:22.790
The implementation
of S/MIME standard

00:38:22.790 --> 00:38:24.680
mandates that
certificates associated

00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:28.640
with users sending emails
are hosted in a public CA.

00:38:28.640 --> 00:38:31.100
If you have this requirement
as a Gmail admin,

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:32.900
you can configure
Gmail to enable S/MIME

00:38:32.900 --> 00:38:35.510
for outgoing emails, set
up policies for content

00:38:35.510 --> 00:38:37.820
and attachment
compliance, and create

00:38:37.820 --> 00:38:39.891
routing rules for incoming
and outgoing emails.

00:38:42.657 --> 00:38:46.160
The last additional protection
we'll quickly cover is Istio.

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:47.710
Istio is an open
source service mesh

00:38:47.710 --> 00:38:50.470
that provides a uniform way to
connect, monitor, and secure

00:38:50.470 --> 00:38:51.700
services.

00:38:51.700 --> 00:38:54.439
The trend towards microservices
and containerization in general

00:38:54.439 --> 00:38:56.230
is leading to more
complicated applications

00:38:56.230 --> 00:38:58.007
that are more
difficult to monitor

00:38:58.007 --> 00:39:00.340
and why I'm excited about
container security these days.

00:39:00.340 --> 00:39:02.120
Sorry, Il-Sung.

00:39:02.120 --> 00:39:05.160
Istio supports managing traffic
flows between microservices,

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:07.154
enforcing access
policies, and aggregating

00:39:07.154 --> 00:39:09.070
telemetry data, and all
of that without having

00:39:09.070 --> 00:39:10.153
to change our application.

00:39:10.153 --> 00:39:12.670
So it's a complete
godsend in that regard.

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:14.754
Given Google's
experience with ALTS,

00:39:14.754 --> 00:39:17.170
Istio is the logical extension
to what we've already built

00:39:17.170 --> 00:39:19.240
and what we've done in
developing microservices

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:20.830
for over 15 years.

00:39:20.830 --> 00:39:23.410
Like ALTS, Istio
issues an identity,

00:39:23.410 --> 00:39:25.560
which is a certificate,
for every service running

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:26.650
in the mesh.

00:39:26.650 --> 00:39:29.650
Istio then transparently adds
Mutual TLS to all service

00:39:29.650 --> 00:39:33.520
calls based on these identities
providing authentication

00:39:33.520 --> 00:39:35.835
and encryption
across your services.

00:39:35.835 --> 00:39:37.960
There are lots of talks at
this conference on Istio

00:39:37.960 --> 00:39:39.190
so I won't dig into it.

00:39:39.190 --> 00:39:41.030
There was one yesterday
on Istio security,

00:39:41.030 --> 00:39:44.150
so check out that
recording online.

00:39:44.150 --> 00:39:46.956
So to sum it all up for
data encryption in transit,

00:39:46.956 --> 00:39:48.330
Google encrypts
and authenticates

00:39:48.330 --> 00:39:50.950
data in transit at one or more
network layers, the network

00:39:50.950 --> 00:39:52.570
layer and the
application layer, when

00:39:52.570 --> 00:39:54.850
data moves outside physical
boundaries not controlled

00:39:54.850 --> 00:39:58.280
by or on behalf of Google.

00:39:58.280 --> 00:39:59.770
And if you want to
learn more, I've

00:39:59.770 --> 00:40:02.280
spent some large amount
of my career writing

00:40:02.280 --> 00:40:03.404
niche whitepapers.

00:40:03.404 --> 00:40:06.070
You can go check them out, learn
more about encryption in trends

00:40:06.070 --> 00:40:08.111
and encryption at rest,
what Il-Sung and I talked

00:40:08.111 --> 00:40:10.087
about today.

00:40:10.087 --> 00:40:11.420
Thank you so much for your time.

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:18.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

